      "html": "<p>Ralf Britz, researcher at the Natural History Museum, London, and one of the leading specialists for this group of fish, answered that these imports are most probably Mastacembelus unicolor or, at least, what is currently thought to be M. unicolor.</p><p>Mastacembelus unicolor was described scientifically in 1831 by Cuvier. The description was based on unpublished data and specimens collected by the naturalists Kuhl and van Hasselt, who died young in Java in 1821 and 1823. They left an important collection of fishes and accurate drawings of fish, including one of Mastacembelus unicolor. It clearly shows the unusual stripe over the pectoral fin and the white seam running along the whole of the dorsal, caudal and ventral fins. The imported fish, apparently a pair, look at least very similar to M. unicolor.</p><p>The pair currently has a light murky skin, which is expected to vanish after the fish have settled.</p>"
    }
